From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LightTouch <lighttouch@localhost>
Date: Sun, 26 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] hw/arm: the frontend's camera into the ISP

The guest's camera path is already here: the A4 ISP model takes commands
through its CPU mailbox, writes NV12 or `yuvs` frames out through dart1, and
iOS renders them. What it writes today is `isp_pattern()`, colour bars. On a
phone there is a real camera a few centimetres away, and the app already has
it open, so the model should hand that through instead.

This is the input counterpart of the display tap, in the shape the microphone
patch established, with one deliberate difference. A microphone ring must not
lose frames -- a recording is a continuous thing and a dropped block is a
click -- so host-mic is a bounded ring that drops only when it is full. A
camera is the opposite: the phone produces 30 frames a second and the ISP
consumes 15, so the newest frame is the only one worth showing and the ones it
replaces are already stale. host-camera is therefore a latest-wins slot per
sensor: a push replaces the untaken frame, `replaced_frames` counts them, and
the guest can never be handed a picture from a third of a second ago. Latency
does not grow with the size of a backlog, because there is no backlog.

Which sensor is not the app's decision, it is the guest's. iOS asks the ISP
which camera it has (`03:00` reads back, `04:00` front), and `isp_command`
records the answer in `s->sensor` and calls host_camera_select(); the app polls
the same value and reopens the phone's matching camera. So the device's own
front/back switch, inside the Camera app, moves the phone's camera, and a
front-camera photo in the guest is a real front-camera photo. The app can also
pin one camera; the engine then ignores the guest's choice and keeps streaming
what it was given.

The taps are asked BEFORE the fallback, in `isp_frame()`. That is the same
ordering the first microphone build got wrong -- a microphone tap placed behind
`driver=none`'s silent voice was never reached and the guest recorded silence
-- so here `host_camera_take()` is tried first and `isp_pattern()` only runs
when the app has pushed nothing. On the Mac, with no app, and with the Android
switch off, that is every frame, and the model behaves exactly as before.

The scaler samples nearest neighbour and takes chroma on even boundaries, so a
chroma sample always covers both pixels of a pair, whatever size the app sent
and whichever of the two output layouts the guest asked for. Frames must be
even-sized NV12 and at most 1280x960; anything else is refused and counted
rather than half-written into the output buffer.

Three buffers per sensor, not two: the guest can hold two frames at once (the one it has not taken and the
one it is copying through the scaler), and a push must fill neither, or a camera faster than the guest
overwrites the picture it is reading -- torn frames, which is what a half-replaced colour plane looks like.
Switching sensors also drops the frame that was waiting: it belongs to the camera the guest just left.

A frame is only written when the guest has acknowledged the one before it. A
sensor switch is a disable, a clear of the output buffers, a mode swap and a
re-enable; in between, the address the model would write to still names buffers
the guest is tearing down, and writing there is writing into memory it may have
reused. Waiting costs a frame at worst and cannot corrupt the guest.

One more way a guest can hang, and the likely reason iOS 6's camera never comes up: a firmware that issues
a command this model does not implement leaves that register set, and the kext polls it for ever. The model
now notices the same write repeated (a poll, not configuration) and clears it, counting how often.

The front module is mounted rotated 180 degrees against the back one, and the
guest draws whatever it is handed, so the front sensor's picture is spun
upright here. On a device the first build showed the front camera's preview
upside down.

The scaler has to be cheap as well as correct: it runs on the host's one vCPU
thread, under the big lock, fifteen times a second, so its cost is time the
guest never gets. The first version divided twice per output pixel and took
19 ms for a 5 MP still on a desktop -- on a phone that is most of a frame and
the emulator looks frozen. A step accumulator replaces the divisions, an
unscaled frame (the common case: the phone's preview is the mode's size) is
copied a row at a time, and the column map is kept between frames: 1.9 ms for
the same still, 0.12 ms for a 640x480 preview. A frame whose size is not a mode
this hardware had is refused instead of being allocated and DMAed, which bounds
both the model's memory and what it can write into the guest.

Two frames are also what broke the first device run: isp_frame() returned
without re-arming its own timer when the block was enabled but had no output
buffer, and switching cameras clears those buffers for a moment. The stream then
stopped for good -- the guest froze on its last picture and the sim hung waiting
for a frame that could never come. The timer now re-arms on that path too; only
the guest's own disable write stops it.

No new device: the ISP model already has all of the guest-visible plumbing,
and the existing slice keeps its pattern assertions with the sensor reads added.

  hw/arm/host-camera.c, include/hw/arm/host-camera.h   the slots, the scaler, counters
  hw/arm/s5l8930_isp.c                                 sensor recognition picks the slot, frames come from it
  contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c/.h             qemu_ios_camera_write/flush/state/sensor/diag (API 2.6)
  hw/arm/meson.build                                   host-camera.c for the S5L8930 family
  tests/slice/ipad1-isp-mailbox.c                      the recognition reads, and that they clear
---
diff --git a/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c b/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c
index d08cfe1..7fcf66d 100644
--- a/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c
+++ b/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.c
@@ -38,6 +38,7 @@
 
 #include "qemu-macos-extras.h"
 #include "hw/misc/ios_vibrator.h"
+#include "hw/arm/host-camera.h"
 #include "hw/arm/host-mic.h"
 
 /*
@@ -1111,3 +1112,102 @@ int qemu_ios_microphone_state(uint32_t *rate, uint32_t *queued, uint64_t *pushed
     }
     return state.capturing ? 1 : 0;
 }
+
+/*
+ * The camera, like the microphone, is a slot the frontend writes on its own thread and the guest's ISP
+ * reads on the vCPU thread: no bottom half, and no frame is ever queued behind another.
+ */
+int qemu_ios_camera_write(int sensor, const void *nv12, int bytes, int width, int height)
+{
+    if (sensor < 0 || sensor >= HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS || !nv12 || bytes <= 0 || width <= 0 || height <= 0) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    return host_camera_push((unsigned)sensor, nv12, (size_t)bytes, (unsigned)width, (unsigned)height) ? 1 : 0;
+}
+
+void qemu_ios_camera_flush(void)
+{
+    host_camera_flush();
+}
+
+/* hw/arm/s5l8930_isp.c keeps how many commands the firmware never took; a stuck one is the classic reason a
+ * guest hangs in a poll, so it belongs in the same report. */
+uint32_t s5l8930_isp_stuck_commands(void);
+
+int qemu_ios_camera_diag(int sensor, int *mode_width, int *mode_height, int *format, int *sensor_active,
+                         unsigned *drawn, unsigned *from_phone, unsigned *unacked, unsigned *no_chroma,
+                         unsigned *stalls, unsigned *stuck_commands)
+{
+    HostCameraState state;
+
+    if (sensor < 0 || sensor >= HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    host_camera_state(&state);
+    if (mode_width) {
+        *mode_width = int(state.sensor[sensor].mode_width);
+    }
+    if (mode_height) {
+        *mode_height = int(state.sensor[sensor].mode_height);
+    }
+    if (format) {
+        *format = int(state.sensor[sensor].format);
+    }
+    if (sensor_active) {
+        *sensor_active = qemu_ios_camera_sensor();
+    }
+    if (drawn) {
+        *drawn = unsigned(state.sensor[sensor].drawn);
+    }
+    if (from_phone) {
+        *from_phone = unsigned(state.sensor[sensor].from_phone);
+    }
+    if (unacked) {
+        *unacked = unsigned(state.sensor[sensor].unacked);
+    }
+    if (no_chroma) {
+        *no_chroma = unsigned(state.sensor[sensor].no_chroma);
+    }
+    if (stalls) {
+        *stalls = unsigned(state.sensor[sensor].stalls);
+    }
+    if (stuck_commands) {
+        *stuck_commands = s5l8930_isp_stuck_commands();
+    }
+    return 1;
+}
+
+int qemu_ios_camera_sensor(void)
+{
+    return host_camera_selected();
+}
+
+int qemu_ios_camera_state(int sensor, uint32_t *width, uint32_t *height, uint64_t *pushed,
+                          uint64_t *taken, uint64_t *replaced, uint64_t *rejected)
+{
+    HostCameraState state;
+
+    if (sensor < 0 || sensor >= HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    host_camera_state(&state);
+    if (width) {
+        *width = state.sensor[sensor].width;
+    }
+    if (height) {
+        *height = state.sensor[sensor].height;
+    }
+    if (pushed) {
+        *pushed = state.sensor[sensor].pushed;
+    }
+    if (taken) {
+        *taken = state.sensor[sensor].taken;
+    }
+    if (replaced) {
+        *replaced = state.sensor[sensor].replaced;
+    }
+    if (rejected) {
+        *rejected = state.sensor[sensor].rejected;
+    }
+    return state.sensor[sensor].width ? 1 : 0;
+}
diff --git a/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.h b/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.h
index 4e31244..fa23e30 100644
--- a/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.h
+++ b/contrib/macos-app/qemu-macos-extras.h
@@ -92,6 +92,32 @@ void qemu_ios_microphone_flush(void);
  * began; every out pointer may be NULL. */
 int qemu_ios_microphone_state(uint32_t *rate, uint32_t *queued, uint64_t *pushed, uint64_t *dropped);
 
+/* The host's camera into the guest's ISP (hw/arm/host-camera.h): one NV12 frame (a Y plane of width*height,
+ * then interleaved CbCr of width*height/2), tightly packed, `bytes` = width*height*3/2. sensor is 0 for the
+ * back camera and 1 for the front, the two the A4's ISP has. Latest frame wins: a push replaces one the guest
+ * has not taken yet, so a guest that runs slower than the camera shows the newest picture and never replays an
+ * old one. Returns 1 when the frame was taken, 0 when it was refused (unknown sensor, odd size, a size above
+ * HOST_CAMERA_MAX_*, or a byte count that does not match). While nothing is pushed the guest shows the model's
+ * test pattern, exactly as before. Any thread. API 2.5. */
+int qemu_ios_camera_write(int sensor, const void *nv12, int bytes, int width, int height);
+/* Forget both sensors' frames: the app stopped capturing, or the guest switched cameras. */
+void qemu_ios_camera_flush(void);
+/* One sensor's counters: the frame waiting for the guest (width/height, 0 when none), and the totals since
+ * the process began. Every out pointer may be NULL. 1 when a frame is waiting, else 0. */
+int qemu_ios_camera_state(int sensor, uint32_t *width, uint32_t *height, uint64_t *pushed,
+                          uint64_t *taken, uint64_t *replaced, uint64_t *rejected);
+/* The sensor the guest's driver last recognised: 0 back, 1 front. The frontend streams that one, since a
+ * phone cannot usefully run both cameras at once. Any thread. */
+int qemu_ios_camera_sensor(void);
+/* What the guest's ISP model is doing with this sensor's frames, for the app's own diagnosis: the mode it
+ * asked for, the layout (0x11 NV12, 0x10 4:2:2), how many frames were drawn, how many came from the phone,
+ * how many were skipped waiting for the guest to acknowledge the previous one, how many wanted a chroma
+ * buffer the guest never named, and how many found no output buffer at all. Any of the outputs may be NULL.
+ * Returns 1 when the sensor exists. Only a running guest has anything to say. */
+int qemu_ios_camera_diag(int sensor, int *mode_width, int *mode_height, int *format, int *sensor_active,
+                         unsigned *drawn, unsigned *from_phone, unsigned *unacked, unsigned *no_chroma,
+                         unsigned *stalls, unsigned *stuck_commands);
+
 /* Queue UTF-8 text for the guest pasteboard (machine property "pasteboard"). */
 void qemu_ios_ui_paste(const char *utf8);
 /* The text last copied inside the guest (UTF-8, at most 64 KiB - 1 bytes: the guest agent's cap), when the
@@ -127,7 +153,7 @@ const char *qemu_ios_build_id(void);
 /* This C API's version, major << 16 | minor. A minor bump only adds entry points;
  * a major bump removes or changes one, and a host built for another major refuses
  * the dylib. */
-#define QEMU_IOS_API_VERSION ((2u << 16) | 4u)
+#define QEMU_IOS_API_VERSION ((2u << 16) | 6u)
 uint32_t qemu_ios_api_version(void);
 
 /* Machine controls. */
diff --git a/hw/arm/host-camera.c b/hw/arm/host-camera.c
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..fa82d3f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/hw/arm/host-camera.c
@@ -0,0 +1,215 @@
+/*
+ * The frontend's camera: the latest frame per sensor, and nothing else.
+ *
+ * See include/hw/arm/host-camera.h for why this is a latest-wins slot rather
+ * than the microphone's ring. The frontend pushes from its own thread (the
+ * camera thread on the phone), the guest's ISP takes from the vCPU thread, and
+ * the lock is held only long enough to swap a pointer and copy one frame.
+ *
+ * Two buffers per sensor, so the frame the guest is reading cannot be
+ * overwritten under it by the next push.
+ *
+ * SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+ */
+#include "qemu/osdep.h"
+#include "hw/arm/host-camera.h"
+
+#include <pthread.h>
+
+/* Three buffers, and two of them can be held at once: the frame the guest has not taken yet, and the one
+ * it is copying right now (it takes a frame, then spends a while in the ISP's scaler with it). A push never
+ * fills either, so a camera faster than the guest cannot overwrite the picture it is reading -- which with
+ * two buffers it could, and the guest drew torn frames where a colour plane had been half replaced. */
+#define HOST_CAMERA_BUFFERS 3
+
+typedef struct HostCameraSlot {
+    uint8_t *buffer[HOST_CAMERA_BUFFERS];
+    size_t capacity[HOST_CAMERA_BUFFERS];
+    int write;                  /* where the next push goes */
+    int pending;                /* the buffer the guest has not taken, -1 when there is none */
+    int busy;                   /* the buffer the guest took last and may still be reading, -1 when none */
+    unsigned width, height;
+    HostCameraSensorState state;
+} HostCameraSlot;
+
+static pthread_mutex_t host_camera_lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
+static unsigned host_camera_active = HOST_CAMERA_BACK;
+static HostCameraSlot host_camera_slots[HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS] = {
+    { .write = 0, .pending = -1, .busy = -1 },
+    { .write = 0, .pending = -1, .busy = -1 },
+};
+
+/* The next buffer a push may fill: not the one waiting for the guest, not the one it is reading. */
+static int host_camera_free_buffer(const HostCameraSlot *s)
+{
+    for (int i = 1; i <= HOST_CAMERA_BUFFERS; i++) {
+        int candidate = (s->write + i) % HOST_CAMERA_BUFFERS;
+
+        if (candidate != s->pending && candidate != s->busy) {
+            return candidate;
+        }
+    }
+    return -1;              /* cannot happen with three buffers and two holders */
+}
+
+static size_t host_camera_bytes(unsigned width, unsigned height)
+{
+    return (size_t)width * height + (size_t)width * height / 2;
+}
+
+bool host_camera_push(unsigned sensor, const uint8_t *nv12, size_t bytes, unsigned width,
+                      unsigned height)
+{
+    HostCameraSlot *s;
+    size_t need;
+
+    if (sensor >= HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS) {
+        return false;
+    }
+    s = &host_camera_slots[sensor];
+    need = (!(width & 1) && !(height & 1) && width && height &&
+            width <= HOST_CAMERA_MAX_WIDTH && height <= HOST_CAMERA_MAX_HEIGHT)
+           ? host_camera_bytes(width, height) : 0;
+
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&host_camera_lock);
+    if (!nv12 || !need || bytes < need) {
+        s->state.rejected++;
+        pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+        return false;
+    }
+    {
+        int target = host_camera_free_buffer(s);
+
+        if (target < 0) {
+            s->state.rejected++;
+            pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+            return false;
+        }
+        if (s->capacity[target] < need) {
+            s->buffer[target] = g_renew(uint8_t, s->buffer[target], need);
+            s->capacity[target] = need;
+        }
+        memcpy(s->buffer[target], nv12, need);
+        if (s->pending >= 0) {
+            s->state.replaced++;    /* the guest never asked for the frame this one replaces */
+        }
+        s->pending = target;
+        s->write = target;
+    }
+    s->width = width;
+    s->height = height;
+    s->state.width = width;
+    s->state.height = height;
+    s->state.pushed++;
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+    return true;
+}
+
+const uint8_t *host_camera_take(unsigned sensor, unsigned *width, unsigned *height)
+{
+    HostCameraSlot *s;
+    const uint8_t *frame;
+
+    if (sensor >= HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS) {
+        return NULL;
+    }
+    s = &host_camera_slots[sensor];
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&host_camera_lock);
+    if (s->pending < 0) {
+        pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+        return NULL;
+    }
+    frame = s->buffer[s->pending];
+    if (width) {
+        *width = s->width;
+    }
+    if (height) {
+        *height = s->height;
+    }
+    s->busy = s->pending;       /* the caller copies out of this one; pushes leave it alone */
+    s->pending = -1;
+    s->state.width = 0;
+    s->state.height = 0;
+    s->state.taken++;
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+    return frame;
+}
+
+void host_camera_flush(void)
+{
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&host_camera_lock);
+    for (unsigned i = 0; i < HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS; i++) {
+        host_camera_slots[i].pending = -1;
+        host_camera_slots[i].busy = -1;
+        host_camera_slots[i].width = host_camera_slots[i].height = 0;
+        host_camera_slots[i].state.width = host_camera_slots[i].state.height = 0;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+}
+
+void host_camera_state(HostCameraState *out)
+{
+    if (!out) {
+        return;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&host_camera_lock);
+    for (unsigned i = 0; i < HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS; i++) {
+        out->sensor[i] = host_camera_slots[i].state;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+}
+
+void host_camera_note(unsigned sensor, unsigned mode_width, unsigned mode_height, unsigned format,
+                      bool from_phone, bool unacked, bool no_chroma, bool stall)
+{
+    HostCameraSlot *s;
+
+    if (sensor >= HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS) {
+        return;
+    }
+    s = &host_camera_slots[sensor];
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&host_camera_lock);
+    s->state.mode_width = mode_width;
+    s->state.mode_height = mode_height;
+    s->state.format = format;
+    if (stall) {
+        s->state.stalls++;
+    } else if (unacked) {
+        s->state.unacked++;
+    } else {
+        s->state.drawn++;
+        s->state.from_phone += from_phone;
+        s->state.no_chroma += no_chroma;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+}
+
+void host_camera_select(unsigned sensor)
+{
+    if (sensor >= HOST_CAMERA_SENSORS) {
+        return;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&host_camera_lock);
+    if (host_camera_active != sensor) {
+        /* The guest is switching cameras: the frame waiting for it belongs to the sensor it just left, and
+         * the app is still sending that one until it polls and reopens. Dropping it here is what keeps the
+         * first frame after a switch from being the old camera's picture. */
+        HostCameraSlot *slot = &host_camera_slots[host_camera_active];
+
+        slot->pending = -1;
+        slot->width = slot->height = 0;
+        slot->state.width = slot->state.height = 0;
+        host_camera_active = sensor;
+    }
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+}
+
+int host_camera_selected(void)
+{
+    int sensor;
+
+    pthread_mutex_lock(&host_camera_lock);
+    sensor = (int)host_camera_active;
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&host_camera_lock);
+    return sensor;
+}
